t-louis-blues-1929.mp4", "mtime": "1712325979", "size": "64730", "md5": "23d5056304a74efb25a5965cd022ce39", "crc32": "fc1d2412", "sha1": "a8c13044c7fd1bce24428bcd74d640cbdf9d9ab2"}, {"name": "st-louis-blues-1929_archive.torrent", "source": "metadata", "btih": "b1e825f4b9586dc303af0b56d2e92aba8a9a27c8", "mtime": "1762317318", "size": "24134", "md5": "7373acffb631bf72151704eea5ee6958", "crc32": "900fd7a0", "sha1": "1d8ff0f9fa277e3e9750c980f5c3ff90cdb4a32a", "format": "Archive BitTorrent"}, {"name": "st-louis-blues-1929_files.xml", "source": "original", "format": "Metadata", "md5": "8b15ea6f26be2130435d3cdc42b77555", "summation": "md5"}, {"name": "st-louis-blues-1929_meta.sqlite", "source": "original", "mtime": "1712325185", "size": "45056", "md5": "1d0f03d73b6333a2a3466e13ee645bfa", "crc32": "fbcea8ae", "sha1": "e4aa37cd220ffc5cb7fe0b639d656ece36dd17a2", "format": "Metadata"}, {"name": "st-louis-blues-1929_meta.xml", "source": "original", "mtime": "1762317317", "size": "9573", "md5": "8b292cd64f54aeabee1765e5f6f8326c", "crc32": "348f443d", "sha1": "db26ae9c56414b7b2a3f358a1a85522394349eb7", "format": "Metadata"}], "files_count": 23, "item_last_updated": 1762317319, "item_size": 2277514702, "metadata": {"identifier": "st-louis-blues-1929", "mediatype": "movies", "collection": ["colorized-movies", "feature_films", "moviesandfilms"], "creator": "Dudley Murphy", "date": "1929", "description": "<div>St. Louis Blues is a 1929 American negro movie film in black and white, colorized, using google colab and GitHub deoldify.\u00a0</div><div><br /></div><div>Appendix:</div><div><br /></div><div><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\"><i><b>St. Louis Blues</b></i>\u00a0is a 1929 American two-reel\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short_film\" title=\"Short film\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">short film</a>\u00a0starring\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessie_Smith\" title=\"Bessie Smith\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Bessie Smith</a>. The early\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound_film\" title=\"Sound film\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">sound film</a>\u00a0features Smith in an\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African-American\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"African-American\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">African-American</a>\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speakeasy\" title=\"Speakeasy\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">speakeasy</a>\u00a0of the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prohibition\" title=\"Prohibition\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">prohibition</a>\u00a0era singing the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._C._Handy\" title=\"W. C. Handy\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">W. C. Handy</a>\u00a0standard, \"<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(song)\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"St. Louis Blues (song)\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">St. Louis Blues</a>\". Directed by\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dudley_Murphy\" title=\"Dudley Murphy\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Dudley Murphy</a>, it is the only known film of Bessie Smith, and the soundtrack is her only recording not controlled by\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_Records\" title=\"Columbia Records\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Columbia Records</a>.</p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\">Bessie Smith had a hit on the song in 1925 and Handy himself asked Bessie Smith to appear in the movie. Handy co-authored the film and was the musical director. The film was a dramatization of the song, a woman left alone by her roving man. It features a band that included\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_P._Johnson\" title=\"James P. Johnson\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">James P. Johnson</a>\u00a0on piano,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Morris_(musician)\" title=\"Thomas Morris (musician)\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Thomas Morris</a>\u00a0and\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Smith_(musician)\" title=\"Joe Smith (musician)\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Joe Smith</a>\u00a0on cornet,\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Addison\" title=\"Bernard Addison\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Bernard Addison</a>\u00a0on guitar and banjo, as well as the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hall_Johnson\" title=\"Hall Johnson\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Hall Johnson Choir</a>\u00a0with some thrilling harmonies at the end.</p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\">The film has an all\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African-American\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"African-American\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">African-American</a>\u00a0cast. Bessie Smith co-stars with Jimmy Mordecai as the boyfriend and Isabel Washington as the other woman.<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-Smith-1\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[1]</a></sup></p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\">It was filmed in June 1929 in\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astoria,_Queens\" title=\"Astoria, Queens\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Astoria, Queens</a>.<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-Smith-1\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[1]</a></sup>\u00a0The film is about 16 minutes long. In 2006, this version was selected for preservation in the United States\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Film_Registry\" title=\"National Film Registry\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">National Film Registry</a>\u00a0by the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Congress\" title=\"Library of Congress\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">Library of Congress</a>\u00a0as being \"culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant\".<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-2\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[2]</a></sup><sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-3\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[3]</a></sup></p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\">The film was rumored for a long time to have been banned as demeaning and to have become\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_film\" title=\"Lost film\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">lost</a>.<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-Smith-1\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[1]</a></sup>\u00a0Neither rumor was true, but when a print was found in Mexico in the mid-1940s, the event was treated as a significant development, even though copies had, in fact, been available elsewhere.<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-Smith-1\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[1]</a></sup>\u00a0\"In 1950, a group of white liberals petitioned the NAACP to buy and destroy the print found in Mexico, which they believed to be the only copy extant.\"<sup class=\"reference\" style=\"line-height:1;font-size:11.2px;\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)#cite_note-Smith-1\" style=\"color:rgb(51,102,204);background:none;\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">[1]</a></sup></p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Blues_(1929_film)\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\">St. Louis Blues (1929 film) - Wikipedia</a><br /></p><p style=\"margin:0.5em 0px 0px;padding-bottom:0.5em;color:rgb(32,33,34);font-family:sans-serif;font-size:14px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);\"><br /></p></div>", "language": "eng", "licenseurl": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/", "scanner": "Internet Archive HTML5 Uploader 1.7.0", "subject": ["St Louis Blues", "1929", "Bessie Smith", "Queen of the Blues", "movie film", "negro", "black american", "african american"], "title": "St. Louis Blues, 1929, Film", "uploader": "markah2020@protonmail.com", "publicdate": "2024-04-05 13:51:34", "addeddate": "2024-04-05 13:51:34", "curation": "[curator]validator@archive.org[/curator][date]20240405135413[/date][comment]checked for malware[/comment]", "collection_added": ["feature_films", "moviesandfilms"]}, "server": "dn600203.us.archive.org", "simplelists": {"solo_manifest": {"solo_manifest_dn600203": {"sys_changed_by": {"source": "task", "task_id": "5424960609"}, "sys_last_changed": "2026-06-08 13:10:57.911175"}}}, "solo": true, "uniq": 538210824, "workable_servers": ["dn600203.us.archive.org"]}